        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A laptop power bank is not a phone power bank with a bigger number on the box. The number that decides whether it works is the wattage on its USB-C port, and the number that decides how long it works is watt-hours, not milliamp-hours. Get those two right and a pack in your bag turns a dead laptop into another full workday; get them wrong and you carry a heavy brick that charges your laptop slower than you drain it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="wattage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Laptop Wattage vs. USB-C PD Output
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the charger that came with your laptop. The wattage printed on it is the most your laptop will ever ask for, and it is the single best guide to the power bank you need. A thin-and-light laptop such as a MacBook Air or a 13-inch Windows ultrabook typically ships with a 30 to 65 watt charger. A 14-inch performance laptop usually comes with something in the 65 to 100 watt range, and a 16-inch MacBook Pro ships with a 140 watt charger. Gaming laptops often use 180 to 330 watt barrel chargers that no power bank can match.
          </p>

          <p>
            Laptop power banks talk to laptops through USB Power Delivery, or PD. The bank and laptop negotiate a voltage and current, and the result sets the wattage. Classic USB PD tops out at 100 watts, delivered at 20 volts and 5 amps. The newer PD 3.1 Extended Power Range adds higher voltages, 28, 36 and 48 volts, which is how a port reaches 140 watts or more. A bank advertised at 140 watts on one port needs both a laptop and a cable that support those higher levels to deliver it; otherwise it falls back to 100 watts or less.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the difference between total output and per-port output. A bank sold as 200 or 300 watts is usually quoting the combined output of every port at once. The most any single port can deliver is often lower, commonly 100 or 140 watts. That is still enough for almost any USB-C laptop, but it means a 300-watt bank will not charge one laptop at 300 watts. It means it can charge a laptop and a tablet and a phone simultaneously without throttling. Read the per-port figure first.
          </p>

          <p>
            A bank does not need to match your charger exactly. If your laptop came with a 96 watt charger and you use a 65 watt bank, the laptop will still charge, more slowly, and it may hold steady rather than gain charge under a heavy workload such as video export. Some laptops show a slow-charger warning, and a few refuse to charge at all below a certain wattage. As a rule, aim for a bank whose single-port output is at least two-thirds of your laptop charger's rating, and match it fully if you work hard while unplugged.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match the per-port USB-C wattage to your laptop charger.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Total output is for charging several devices at once. The per-port rating decides whether your laptop charges at full speed, slowly, or not at all. Aim for at least two-thirds of your charger's wattage, and all of it if you work under heavy load.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="mah-wh" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. mAh to Wh: How Many Laptop Charges You Really Get
          </h2>

          <p>
            Power banks are sold in milliamp-hours because the number looks big, but laptop batteries are rated in watt-hours, and that is the unit that lets you compare the two. To convert, multiply the mAh rating by the battery's nominal cell voltage, usually 3.6 or 3.7 volts, and divide by 1,000. A 24,000 mAh bank works out to roughly 86 to 89 Wh. A 20,000 mAh bank is about 72 to 74 Wh, and a 25,000 mAh bank about 90 to 93 Wh. Many makers now print the Wh figure on the case, partly because airlines ask for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            You do not get all of those watt-hours. A laptop bank has to boost its cells from about 3.6 volts to 20 volts or more, and that conversion loses energy as heat. A planning figure of 70 to 85 percent delivered is realistic for a good bank, and the lower end applies at high wattage. So a roughly 86 Wh bank can be expected to put somewhere around 60 to 73 Wh into a laptop. Cheap banks with inflated mAh ratings and poor converters can do considerably worse.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now compare that with your laptop. A 13-inch MacBook Air has a battery in the low 50s of watt-hours; a 14-inch MacBook Pro sits around 70 Wh; and a 16-inch MacBook Pro is right at the 100 Wh legal maximum for a laptop battery. Windows ultrabooks typically land between 50 and 75 Wh. In practice that means a 20,000 to 25,000 mAh bank delivers roughly one full charge of a mid-size laptop, or one and a bit of a small one, and something like two-thirds of a charge for a 16-inch machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            That number surprises people who expect a power bank to triple their battery life. It will not. What it does is roughly double a modern laptop's unplugged day, which for most people is the difference that matters: a full transatlantic flight, a conference day without an outlet, or a full workday during a power outage. If you genuinely need three or four laptop charges, you need more watt-hours than any airline-legal bank can hold, and that is where a small power station comes in.
          </p>

