        <div id="airline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The 100 Wh Airline Limit, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 100 Wh figure that shapes the whole laptop power bank market comes from aviation rules. Under FAA and IATA guidance, spare lithium-ion batteries, which is what a power bank counts as, must travel in carry-on luggage, never in checked bags. Batteries up to 100 Wh are allowed without airline approval. Batteries between 100 and 160 Wh require the airline's approval and are limited to two spares per passenger. Anything above 160 Wh cannot fly as a passenger item at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Airlines and screeners check the watt-hour figure printed on the bank. If the case shows only mAh, they may calculate it themselves or simply refuse the bank, which is a bad way to start a trip. All of the banks in this guide are designed to fall under 100 Wh, and reputable brands print the figure on the casing. If you are buying a bank primarily for travel, confirm that the Wh rating is clearly marked, and avoid banks with ratings that look suspiciously large for their size.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rules are also tightening around using power banks in flight. A growing number of airlines restrict charging devices from a power bank during the flight, require banks to stay visible rather than in an overhead bin, or ban in-flight use altogether, after a series of cabin battery fires. Policies differ by airline and change often, so check your carrier's current rules before you plan on charging a laptop at 35,000 feet. Charging at the gate is almost always fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is that 100 Wh, roughly 26,000 to 27,000 mAh, is the useful ceiling for a laptop power bank. Anything larger is a power station and stays home. That is why our picks cluster in the 20,000 to 26,250 mAh range, and it is also why the step up to a station, which we cover below and in our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station guide</a>, is a step change rather than a slightly bigger bank.
          </p>

