        <div id="passthrough" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Pass-Through Charging and Recharge Speed
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pass-through charging means the bank can charge your devices while it is itself plugged in and charging. It sounds minor, but it changes how you use a laptop bank. At a hotel with one outlet, you plug the bank into the wall and the laptop into the bank, and both charge overnight. During a power outage, when the power flickers back for an hour, the whole stack refills at once. Most current high-output banks from major brands support some form of pass-through, but check the listing because implementations vary.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are caveats. Pass-through generates extra heat and puts more cycles on the bank's cells, so using it every day as a desk charger shortens the bank's life. Some banks also prioritize the connected device and charge themselves only after it is full, while others split the input. For occasional use it is a genuinely useful feature; as a permanent setup, it is better to plug the laptop straight into its own charger and leave the bank for when you need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recharge speed matters more than most buyers expect. An 86 Wh bank that recharges at 20 watts takes most of a day to refill; one that accepts 100 watts or more over USB-C can refill in about an hour or so. That is the difference between a bank that is ready for tomorrow's flight and one that is not. High-output banks like the Anker 737 and Prime accept fast USB-C input, but only from a wall charger that can supply that much, so a cheap 20-watt phone brick will still recharge them slowly.
          </p>

          <p>
            For outage use, fast recharging is even more valuable. When power is intermittent, a bank that can soak up 100 watts or more in short windows is far more useful than one that sips. It also makes the bank a good partner for solar: a folding panel feeding a fast-charging bank or a small power station puts energy away efficiently while the sun is out. Our guides to <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">solar power banks</a> and <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">portable solar panels</a> go into what size panel makes sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="multi-device" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Charging a Laptop, Phone and Tablet at Once
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people carry more than a laptop. A phone, earbuds, a tablet or a camera usually come along, and a laptop power bank with two or three ports can charge them all. This is where total output matters. A 140-watt bank that sends 100 watts to a laptop has only 40 watts left for everything else, and when you plug in a second device, many banks renegotiate every port, briefly cutting power to all of them and then splitting output.
          </p>

          <p>
            Banks with 200 to 300 watts of total output, such as the UGREEN Nexode and the Anker Prime, are built for this situation. They can feed a laptop at close to full speed while still charging a phone quickly. The Anker Prime goes further and can run two large laptops at high wattage simultaneously, which matters for couples traveling with two work machines or for photographers charging a laptop and a camera battery charger at once. For one laptop and one phone, it is more than you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that more ports do not mean more capacity. Charging three devices at once drains a 90 Wh bank three times faster, and the laptop is still the biggest draw. If your goal is to get a laptop through a long flight, unplug the earbuds and tablet until the laptop is done. If your goal is to keep a family's phones going through an outage, a phone-sized bank for each person is often a better plan, as our guide to <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">portable chargers for power outages</a> explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Built-in cables are another quality-of-life detail worth considering. A bank with an attached USB-C cable, like Anker's 25K 165W model, removes the most common failure of all: leaving the right cable at home. Built-in cables are usually rated for 100 watts or so, which suits most laptops. The downside is that a damaged built-in cable can make the whole bank awkward to use, so look for one that also has a standard USB-C port as a fallback.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cables" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cables, E-Markers and Laptop Compatibility
          </h2>

          <p>
            A high-wattage power bank is only as fast as the cable you plug into it. Ordinary USB-C cables are rated for 3 amps, which caps them at 60 watts. To pass more than 60 watts, a cable needs an electronic marker chip, or e-marker, that tells the bank it can carry 5 amps, which unlocks 100 watts at 20 volts. To reach 140 watts or more under PD 3.1, the cable must be rated for 240 watts. Many cables bundled with phones are 60-watt cables, which quietly throttle a laptop bank.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your bank seems to charge your laptop slowly, the cable is the first suspect. Buy a cable explicitly rated for 100 watts, or 240 watts if you have a 140-watt laptop such as a 16-inch MacBook Pro, from a reputable brand, and keep it with the bank. Short cables are also slightly more efficient than long ones. A small USB-C power meter, a cheap inline tester that shows voltage and wattage, is a worthwhile purchase if you want to see exactly what the bank and laptop have negotiated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every laptop charges over USB-C. Many older laptops, budget models and most gaming laptops use a barrel connector, and a USB-C power bank will not charge them directly. Some gaming laptops do accept USB-C charging but limit it to around 100 watts, which keeps the battery topped up during light work but cannot sustain gaming loads. Check your laptop's specifications for USB-C PD charging support and the maximum wattage before buying any bank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, the laptop has a say. Even with the right bank and cable, a laptop under heavy load may draw more than the bank supplies and slowly drain its own battery while plugged in. That is not a fault; the bank is still extending runtime substantially. If you need full performance unplugged for hours, such as rendering video on a 16-inch machine, the honest answer is that a power bank cannot fully keep up, and a station with an AC outlet and your original charger is the better tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When a Small Power Station Beats a Power Bank
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power bank is the right answer for travel, for a single long day, and for most people's outage plans. It stops being the right answer when you need several laptop charges in a row, run equipment with an AC plug, or work remotely for days at a time. At that point the 100 Wh ceiling becomes the limiting factor, and the next step up is a small power station in the 250 to 300 Wh range, which holds roughly three times the energy of the largest legal bank.
          </p>

          <p>
            A DC-only station like the Anker SOLIX C300 DC makes the jump without paying for an inverter you may not need. It has high-wattage USB-C ports that charge laptops directly, which is more efficient than plugging your original laptop charger into an AC outlet, because it skips a conversion from DC to AC and back. For a remote worker in a cabin, a van, or a home office during a long outage, that adds up to three or four full laptop charges from one unit, plus phones and a router.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you also need to run something with a wall plug, such as a monitor, a modem or a CPAP, choose a station with AC outlets. Our guide to the <a href="/best-anker-solix-power-station/">best Anker SOLIX power stations</a> covers the range of sizes, and for anyone keeping a home office online through outages, a desk-side <a href="/best-ups-battery-backup/">UPS battery backup</a> is often the better tool for the desktop and router, since it switches over instantly when the grid drops. For CPAP users, our <a href="/best-battery-backup-for-cpap/">CPAP battery backup guide</a> covers overnight sizing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is portability. A station weighs several times as much as a bank, cannot go on a plane, and is overkill for anyone who only needs to get through a flight or a conference. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">camping power station guide</a> covers stations sized for weekends away, and the main <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station guide</a> compares the full field if your needs stretch from laptops to appliances.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which One to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you want one laptop power bank that handles almost anything, buy the <a href="#anker-737-power-bank">Anker 737 Power Bank</a>. Its 140-watt port can charge even a 16-inch MacBook Pro at full speed, its roughly 86 Wh capacity flies anywhere, and its display shows exactly what is going in and out. It has been on the market long enough to have a solid reputation, which matters with lithium batteries. It is heavy for its size, and that is the main reason not to choose it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you routinely charge two laptops or a laptop and a tablet at full speed, step up to the <a href="#anker-prime-26k-300w">Anker Prime 26K 300W</a>. It is the most capable bank here and priced to match, so buy it for a real need rather than a spec sheet. If you hate carrying cables, the <a href="#anker-laptop-power-bank-25k-165w">Anker Laptop Power Bank 25K 165W</a> with built-in cables is the most convenient travel bank. For a capable multi-port bank at a lower price, the <a href="#ugreen-nexode-25000mah-200w">UGREEN Nexode 25000mAh 200W</a> is a strong value.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your laptop draws 100 watts or less, which covers most 13- and 14-inch machines, and you care about how the bank fits in a laptop sleeve, the <a href="#baseus-blade-100w">Baseus Blade 100W</a> is the slim choice. It holds less than the others, so it is best for one ultrabook charge rather than a big machine. And if you need several laptop charges for remote work or outages, skip the banks and buy the <a href="#anker-solix-c300-dc">Anker SOLIX C300 DC</a> to leave at home or in the car.
          </p>

          <p>
            What we would avoid: no-name banks advertising 30,000 mAh or more with 100-watt output at bargain prices. Inflated capacity claims are common in this category, airline staff may refuse a bank without a clear Wh label, and a cheap lithium pack is the last thing you want in a backpack or bedroom. Buy from a brand with a track record, register the warranty, and retire any bank that swells, gets hot at rest, or has been crushed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on total wattage instead of per-port output</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 200- or 300-watt bank is usually quoting all ports combined. The single USB-C port your laptop uses may top out at 100 or 140 watts. Match the per-port figure to your laptop charger, and treat the total as a measure of how many devices it can feed at once.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a 60-watt cable on a laptop bank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ordinary USB-C cables carry 3 amps, capping them at 60 watts no matter what the bank can do. Laptops above 60 watts need a 5-amp e-marked cable rated for 100 watts, and 140-watt laptops need a 240-watt cable. A wrong cable is the most common cause of slow laptop charging.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting several laptop charges from one bank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Airline-legal banks hold at most 100 Wh, and conversion losses mean a laptop sees roughly 70 to 85 percent of that. Plan on about one full charge for a typical laptop. If you need three or four charges, a small power station is the honest answer, not a bigger-sounding bank.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Packing a power bank in checked luggage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spare lithium batteries, including every power bank, must travel in carry-on bags under FAA and IATA rules. Banks found in checked bags are typically removed and may be confiscated. Keep the bank in your carry-on, make sure its Wh rating is printed on the case, and check your airline's in-flight use policy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every laptop charges over USB-C</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many budget and gaming laptops use barrel connectors and cannot charge from a USB-C bank, and some that do support USB-C cap it near 100 watts. Check your laptop's specifications for USB-C Power Delivery charging and its maximum wattage before buying, or you may own a bank that only charges your phone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best laptop power bank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Anker 737 Power Bank (PowerCore 24K) is the best laptop power bank. Its single USB-C port supports up to 140 watts, enough for nearly any USB-C laptop at full speed, and its roughly 86 Wh capacity flies without airline approval. Choose the Anker Prime 26K if you charge two laptops at once, or the Baseus Blade for a slim ultrabook bank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts does a power bank need to charge a laptop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check the wattage on your laptop's original charger. Ultrabooks usually need 30 to 65 watts, mid-size performance laptops 65 to 100 watts, and 16-inch MacBook Pros up to 140 watts. A bank whose single USB-C port delivers at least two-thirds of that will charge the laptop, more slowly; matching it fully gives full speed under load.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many times will a power bank charge my laptop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually about once. A 20,000 to 26,000 mAh bank holds roughly 72 to 100 Wh, and after conversion losses delivers around 70 to 85 percent of that. Most laptops have 50 to 75 Wh batteries, so expect about one full charge for a typical laptop, a bit more for a small ultrabook, and roughly two-thirds of a charge for a 16-inch machine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I take a laptop power bank on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in carry-on luggage only. Under FAA and IATA rules, power banks up to 100 Wh are allowed without airline approval, banks from 100 to 160 Wh need airline approval and are limited to two, and anything above 160 Wh is prohibited. Power banks are never allowed in checked bags. Some airlines also restrict using banks during the flight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I convert mAh to Wh for a power bank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Multiply the mAh rating by the cell voltage, usually 3.6 or 3.7 volts, then divide by 1,000. A 24,000 mAh bank is about 86 to 89 Wh, and a 20,000 mAh bank about 72 to 74 Wh. Use the watt-hour figure printed on the case when there is one, since airlines and laptop battery ratings both use watt-hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a power bank charge my laptop while I use it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as long as the bank supplies enough wattage for your laptop's workload. Under light use, a 65-watt bank keeps most ultrabooks charging. Under heavy load, such as gaming or video export, a laptop can draw more than the bank delivers and slowly drain while plugged in. The bank still extends runtime substantially, just not indefinitely at full performance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power station better than a laptop power bank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For travel, no; stations cannot fly and are much heavier. For outages, remote work or several laptop charges in a row, yes. A roughly 300 Wh station such as the Anker SOLIX C300 DC holds about three times the energy of the largest legal bank, which means three to four laptop charges plus phones, and the option to recharge from solar.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
